CASE = [
 Q("<b>Case study.</b> A sultan of the Tughlaq dynasty moved his capital from Delhi to Daulatabad, the former Devagiri, hoping to rule the south better. Later he issued token coins of cheap metal and declared them equal in value to silver. Many people forged the coins at home, and traders refused to accept them. (i) To which place was the capital shifted? (ii) Name the sultan. (iii) Why did the token currency fail? (iv) Give two lessons that rulers can learn from these experiments.",
   "(i) Daulatabad (1). (ii) Muhammad bin Tughlaq (1). (iii) It was easy to forge, so trust was lost (1). (iv) Policies need careful planning of logistics and public trust; the new capital was far from the northern base and caused hardship (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q("<b>Case study.</b> Emperor Akbar married princesses of Rajput families and made allies of many Rajput rulers. He abolished the jizya and followed the policy of peace with all. He also had the Mahabharata translated into Persian. (i) What was Akbar's policy of 'peace with all' called? (ii) Which tax did he abolish? (iii) By what name is the Persian Mahabharata known? (iv) How did these steps help to keep the empire stable?",
   "(i) Sulh-i-kul (1). (ii) Jizya (1). (iii) Razmnama (1). (iv) They won the loyalty of Rajputs and non-Muslims and made Indian texts accessible to the court, reducing conflict and strengthening rule (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q("<b>Case study.</b> In 1671 a large Mughal force advanced on the Brahmaputra. The Ahoms, led by Lachit Borphukan, used their knowledge of the river and the terrain. The Ahom paik system, in which men served the state in return for land, gave them a ready army and labour force. The Ahoms won at Saraighat. (i) Who led the Ahoms? (ii) In which year was the battle fought? (iii) On which river was it fought? (iv) Why could the Ahoms defeat a larger Mughal army?",
   "(i) Lachit Borphukan (1). (ii) 1671 (1). (iii) Brahmaputra (1). (iv) They knew the terrain and river, used guerrilla-style tactics, and the paik system provided soldiers and labour (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
]

